A||||4||AusFiero||Temperature gauge malfunction & Overheating problem||||5||
Z||000000||AusFiero||03-20-2001||05:54 PM||||Ok I am sure somebody will know what is causing this. My temperature gauge has stopped working and basically stays in a position of no temperature or trying to go off the dial at the hot end of the gauge, even when the car is running cold.<P>Is this a problem at the engine end, and if so which sensor? Or is this a problem at the dashboard end?<P>The car has also decided to overheat if the air/con isn't left on to keep the fan going.<P>Is the same sensor operating the gauge and making the fan cut in? Or not cut in in this case.<P>The car has had new caps fitted both ends, radiator has been disassembled and professionally reconditioned, Water pump looked in excellent condition when off the car 5000 kms ago and a new 180 degree thermostat has just been fitted.<P>Any help would be appreciated as local mechanics just scratch their heads and charge me lots to finally tell me they don't know a lot about Fieros.<BR>||||reg||5
Z||000001||StuGood||03-20-2001||06:55 PM||||Radiator fan is turned on by a one-pin switch, which has a green wire with white stripe going to it.  Ground the green+white wire, and the fan should turn on. On the V6, this switch is located atop the intake manifold, near the water outlet (the housing that has thermostat in it).  You can leave this wire grounded, until you obtain a replacement temperature switch.  <P>The temperature-gauge sensor is separate.  On  the V6, it's screwed into the aft, left corner of the aft cylinder head.  This sensor has a 2-pin connector.  Many times, this connector's plastic shell crumbles away due to exhaust manifold heat.  One wire turns on a temperature warning light on the dashboard; the other controls the temperature gauge. ||||reg||1
Z||000002||theogre||03-20-2001||07:56 PM||||the overheat sounds like you have air in it. check the heater core and see if it's leaked. It can pinhole and let air into the system wihtout flowing enough coolant out to be obvious.<P>also check the lines under the car. You could have one leaking under there.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Bob Englert||03-20-2001||09:15 PM||||I would also check to make sure you have the right radiator cap.  The Advacne Auto cap I bought was not the right kind (the computer was wrong).  They make both vented and non vented caps.  I think (someone please correct me if I am wrong) you need a non vented cap for the Fiero.<P>On my SE 4cyl. the car ran fine but I parked it in a nose down position and the water ran out of the radiator and overfilled the overfill coolant tank.  This basically created a leak and the next day it badly overheated because about 1/4 of the colant was gone.  I discovered the problem by accident when I filled the system at teh thermostat housing with the overfill tank low and after a while the level at the thermostat was low but the overfill tank was full!  I changed to a NAPA radiator cap and all was good from then on.<P>It does sound like you have air in your coolant system.  Did you follow the fill procedure in your owners manual or Haynes manual?<BR><P>------------------<BR>Bob<BR>2 - 87 GT's<BR>85 SE 4 cyl||||reg||1
Z||000004||Wolfhound||03-20-2001||09:38 PM||||You can check your gauge and warning light by individually grounding the two wires to the temp sensor to the engine. One will peg the guage the other will opperate the warning light. If they work and your getting false readings it,s the sensor.||||reg||1
